- pointer-atom-index-ignores-leading-zero (include/utils.h:753,
  js_atom_is_index): treats any atom whose string form parses as a
  non-negative decimal (e.g. "01") as an array index, so
  Pointer#toString()/toRFC6901()/atoms_serialize() render it as an
  index (`[1]` / `/1`) instead of the literal string key ("01"),
  losing the leading zero. ECMAScript's array-index canonical form (and
  RFC 6901's array-index grammar) both require no leading zero except
  for "0" itself, so "01" should stay a plain string key. Shared by
  every module that reads array-index-ness off an atom via
  js_atom_is_index, not pointer-specific. Found while adding RFC 6901
  support to quickjs-pointer.c/src/pointer.c.

    import { Pointer } from 'pointer';
    Pointer.from(["01", "x"]).toString();
    // "[1].x" -- should be ".01.x" (leading zero silently dropped)

- iohandler-fallback-wrong-module-loader (src/utils.c:3318, js_iohandler_fn):
  when `globalThis.os` (or the given global_obj name) isn't set, the
  fallback path calls quickjs-libc's raw `js_module_loader()`, which
  treats the module name as a filesystem path and tries to open a file
  literally named "os" - it doesn't know about qjsm's builtin module
  table (that's `jsm_module_loader()` in src/qjsm.c, not reachable from
  shared code in utils.c). Always throws "'os' module required" even
  though the 'os' module is a real builtin and may already be imported
  by name elsewhere in the same script. Found after flipping qjsm's
  load_std default to 0 (std/os no longer auto-global) - this fallback
  was previously dead code because globalThis.os always existed.
  Affects any C code path that calls js_iohandler_fn() (native async
  socket read/write registration, at least) when run without --std.

    // qjsm, without --std:
    import * as os from 'os';
    import { AsyncSocket } from 'sockets';
    const s = new AsyncSocket(...);
    await s.recv(...); // ReferenceError: 'os' module required

- excluded-native-modules-unreachable-without-shared-build (CMakeLists.txt):
  BUILD_SHARED_MODULES defaults OFF, so a from-scratch build never produces
  the .so files that BUILTINS_NATIVE_EXCLUDE-excluded native modules
  (lexer, location, repeater, tree-walker, child-process) or a module
  dropped from BUILTINS_COMPILED (e.g. stack) would need for qjsm's
  runtime dynamic-load fallback -- import of those names fails outright.
  test_lexer.js/test_location.js/test_repeater.js/test_treewalker.js/
  test_childprocess.js/test_stack.js all pass in build/x86_64-linux-gnu
  today only because that directory still has stale .so files left over
  from an earlier build configuration (mtimes predate the current
  CMakeLists.txt logic) -- they are not regenerated by a current build,
  and a genuinely fresh build directory (e.g. build/x86_64-linux-bundle)
  fails all six tests. Found while setting up the bundle build.

    cmake -B build/fresh -S . && cmake --build build/fresh
    ctest --test-dir build/fresh -R test_lexer.js  # fails: could not load module 'lexer'

- pigpio-submodule-unused (CMakeLists.txt:26,389-398,742-744;
  quickjs-gpio.c): the `pigpio` git submodule is vendored
  (.gitmodules) and README/Building's gating table implies `gpio`
  needs an external GPIO library, but `quickjs-gpio.c` never includes
  any pigpio header (it does direct memory-mapped register access
  instead), and every CMakeLists.txt reference to pigpio/BUILD_PIGPIO
  is commented out. The submodule is dead weight as currently wired up.
  Found while fact-checking the README intro paragraph's claim that
  qjs-modules wraps pigpio.

    grep -n '#include' quickjs-gpio.c   # no pigpio.h
    grep -n 'pigpio' CMakeLists.txt     # every hit is commented out

- dom-wrapper-property-access-cost-per-level (lib/dom.js:2117-2119, `Element`
  `children` getter, `Factory.for(this).NodeList.cache(...)`): once the
  parser-depth bug and querySelectorAll's ancestor-check were both fixed,
  document.querySelectorAll('A') on a real ~16MB bookmarks export (22892
  <A> tags, tree depth now correctly bounded at 18) was still slow.
  Benchmarked with perf_hooks.now() over the same 1000 matched paths, four
  ways: applyPath (lib/dom.js:101-122, a plain JS `obj = obj[k]` loop)
  against the live wrapper averaged 9.53ms/call; native Pointer.deref()
  (src/pointer.c:643, pointer_deref) against the same live wrapper
  averaged 11.03ms/call - *slower*, not faster, ruling out "JS loop
  overhead" as the cause. Both against the raw object instead (bypassing
  Node/Element wrapper construction entirely) dropped to 0.018ms/call
  (applyPath) and 0.0014ms/call (deref) - a ~500-1000x difference from the
  same two traversal methods on the same paths, isolating the cost
  entirely to the live wrapper's property access chain, not to
  applyPath's implementation or to using JS vs native C for the walk.
  Each `obj[k]` on a live wrapper goes through Proxy-backed machinery
  (`children` getter above, and/or other Factory/NodeList/Collection
  Proxies) that is expensive enough per call, at every path level, to
  dominate everything else; not yet root-caused which specific step
  (NodeList construction, Factory.cache, or the per-element Proxy in
  Collection/NodeList) is responsible, or whether it's fixable by
  memoizing/caching more aggressively vs. inherent to the Proxy design.

    import { performance } from 'perf_hooks';
    import { Parser } from 'dom';
    const doc = new Parser().parseFromFile('bookmarks.html', 'utf-8'); // real, large, deep-ish document
    const t0 = performance.now();
    const nodes = [...doc.querySelectorAll('A')];
    console.log(performance.now() - t0, nodes.length); // ~9-11ms per match,
                                                         // dominated by wrapper
                                                         // property access

- getopt-long-option-after-short-misparsed (lib/util.js:1336,1368-1371,
  getOpt): `short`/`long` are declared once at the top of getOpt() and never
  reset per-argument, only ever set to `true`. Once any short option
  (`-x`) has been parsed, `short` stays `true` for the rest of the call, so
  a later long option (`--foo`) still takes the short-option branch
  (`start = short ? 1 : 2` stays 1, `end = short ? 2 : ...` stays 2) -
  its name is computed as `arg.substring(1, 2)`, i.e. just the literal
  `-` character, which never matches any real option name. The whole
  `--foo` token then falls through unrecognized into the positional
  ('@') bucket instead of being applied, and the option's own default/
  absence goes unnoticed since getOpt doesn't report unrecognized flags.
  Only argument *order* determines whether this happens - the same long
  option before any short option works fine. Found while testing
  utilities/runtime-diff.js's `-g <name> ... --all` (documented order in
  its own --help) silently dropping `--all` and mis-capturing it as a
  positional module name.

    import { getOpt } from 'util';
    const OPTIONS = { g: [true, null, 'g'], all: [false, null, 'a'], '@': 'names' };
    getOpt(OPTIONS, ['-g', 'Math', '--all', 'foo']);
    // { "@": ["--all", "foo"], "g": "Math" } -- 'all' key missing entirely,
    // '--all' misfiled as a positional
    getOpt(OPTIONS, ['--all', '-g', 'Math', 'foo']);
    // { "@": ["foo"], "all": true, "g": "Math" } -- correct, only the
    // argument order differs

- lib-tty-imports-nonexistent-fdopensync (lib/tty.js:3): `import { fdopenSync,
  out } from 'std'` imports a `fdopenSync` export that doesn't exist - `std`
  only has `fdopen` (js_std_fdopen, quickjs-libc.c:915). This breaks loading
  the `tty` module entirely (`import('tty')`/`import('node:tty')` throws).
  Found while running utilities/runtime-diff.js against node:tty on qjsm.

    import('tty');
    // ReferenceError/SyntaxError: Could not find export 'fdopenSync' in
    // module 'std'

