  update 2: the segfaults above were against the stale build/x86_64-linux-gnu
  (see jsm-module-loader note elsewhere in this file / test-deep.js's finding
  that that directory's .so files predate the current qjsm and match no
  current CMake target); against build/bt-2026, the finished
  tests/unittests/test-child-process.js passes 5/5 identical runs
  ("7 tests succeeded.", exit 0). It does leave one deterministic (5/5),
  harmless artifact: an extra "TypeError: not a function at wait (native)"
  is printed to stderr *after* "7 tests succeeded." on every run, sourced
  from the `kill()` test's `child.wait()` call - despite that call's own
  result being used successfully inside the test (the OK is logged first).
  Looks like a duplicate/late invocation of `wait` from the module's async
  job queue, not something the test itself does twice. Doesn't affect the
  exit code or tinytest's pass count; not root-caused further.

    for i in 1 2 3 4 5; do
      QUICKJS_MODULE_PATH="build/bt-2026;lib" build/bt-2026/qjsm tests/unittests/test-child-process.js
    done   # "7 tests succeeded." each time, but a stray TypeError trails every run

- textdecoder-utf16-decode-returns-null (quickjs-textcode.c:66-89): calling
  `TextDecoder('utf-16').decode()` (or 'utf-16be') on well-formed input
  always returns `null` instead of the decoded string, 100% reproducible
  (not flaky). UTF-8 and UTF-32 decode work fine on equivalent input via the
  same function (textdecoder_decode, quickjs-textcode.c:46), so the bug is
  specific to the `case UTF16:` branch; not root-caused further (candidate
  culprits: `u16[1]` is left uninitialized when `libutf_c16_type(u16[0])`
  says the code unit isn't a surrogate, which is then still passed into
  `libutf_c16_to_c32(u16, &cp)`, or `dbuf_encode` silently not appending).
  TextEncoder with 'utf-16' works fine (produces a Uint16Array via
  encode()). Routed around in tests/unittests/test-textcode.js, which only
  exercises UTF-16 on the encode() side.

    import { TextDecoder } from 'textcode';
    new TextDecoder('utf-16').decode(new Uint8Array([97, 0])); // null, expected "a"

- deepiterator-never-descends-plain-object-root (quickjs-deep.c:314-411):
  `new DeepIterator(root)` / `deep.iterate(root)` never recurses into `root`
  when `root` is a plain object - `next()` reports `done: true` on the very
  first call, 100% reproducible, even with an explicit always-true
  predicate. An array root works fine (yields each element, recursing into
  nested arrays/objects one level and even further for nested arrays). A
  plain object nested *inside* an array root is yielded once as a whole
  value but is itself never descended into either. `deep.find`/
  `deep.select`/`deep.forEach`/`deep.iterate`-as-used-by-those (which all
  push the same way via `property_recursion_push`) traverse plain objects
  correctly, so the bug is specific to `js_deep_iterator_next`'s extra
  `if(property_enumeration_length(penum) == 0) continue;` check
  (quickjs-deep.c:347), which find/select/forEach don't have - suspect
  `property_enumeration_length` reads 0 for a freshly-pushed plain-object
  frame before its first `next`, causing an immediate skip/pop instead of
  descending. Also, `DeepIterator.prototype.skip()` (quickjs-deep.c:442-445)
  ends iteration entirely instead of resuming at the next sibling: after
  skipping element 0 of `[[1, 2], 3]`, the following `next()` reports
  `done: true` rather than yielding `3`. Not root-caused further. Worked
  around in tests/unittests/test-deep.js by exercising `DeepIterator`/
  `deep.iterate()` only over array roots, and by only asserting that
  `skip()` prevents the skipped subtree's own values from ever being
  yielded (not that iteration continues afterward).

    import { DeepIterator } from 'deep';
    new DeepIterator({ a: 1 }, () => true).next(); // {done: true}, expected {value: [1, ['a']], done: false}

    import { DeepIterator } from 'deep';
    const it = new DeepIterator([[1, 2], 3]);
    it.next();     // {value: [[1, 2], [0]], done: false}
    it.skip();
    it.next();     // {done: true}, expected {value: [3, [1]], done: false}

- pointer-splice-insert-throws-garbage-exception (quickjs-pointer.c:501-518):
  `Pointer.prototype.splice(start, end, ...atomsToInsert)` works fine as a
  pure deletion (no atoms to insert: it mutates in place and returns the
  removed sub-pointer, like Array.prototype.splice), but as soon as any
  insertion atoms are passed, `pointer_splice(...)` (called with `atoms`
  built by `js_argv_to_atoms`) returns NULL, and js_pointer_method1's tail
  (`return ret ? js_pointer_wrap(ctx, ret) : JS_EXCEPTION;`, line 549) then
  returns the `JS_EXCEPTION` sentinel without ever calling `JS_Throw()` -
  i.e. pointer_splice fails without setting a real pending exception. The
  resulting "exception" is garbage: catching it in JS and trying to read/
  print it throws a secondary `ReferenceError: arg is not initialized` from
  inside the engine. 100% reproducible, not flaky. Routed around in
  tests/unittests/test-pointer.js's splice() test by only exercising the
  deletion form.

    import { Pointer } from 'pointer';
    const p = new Pointer(['a', 'b', 0]);
    try { p.splice(0, 0, 'z'); } catch(e) { console.log(e); } // secondary crash printing e

- misc-classid-struct-scraping-segfault (src/utils.c:1678-1759):
  `misc.classId(name)`, `misc.className(id)` and `misc.classAtom(id)` are
  all unsafe to call - `classId()` alone segfaults reliably (10/10 runs)
  once called two or three times in the same script (e.g.
  `classId('Array'); classId('Object'); classId('X')`), and
  `className`/`classAtom` on any id it returns segfault 100% of the time,
  even from a single call. Root cause: `js_class_count()`
  (src/utils.c:1678-1683) reads the live class count via a hardcoded byte
  offset into the opaque `JSRuntime` struct - `*((uint32_t*)rt +
  DEF6432(27, 16))` - i.e. it "struct-scrapes" a private quickjs-internal
  layout instead of using a public accessor. `js_class_atom`/`js_class_id`/
  `js_class_find` (same file, right below) do the same for the class array
  pointer. On the currently vendored QuickJS this offset no longer lines
  up with the real `class_count` field, so `js_class_count()` returns a
  garbage huge number (observed: 7980 for `classId('Array')`, where a
  real class count is more like 100-200); `js_class_find()` then walks
  `class_count` entries through `class_arr`, reading far past the real
  array - which is why even repeated `classId()` calls alone eventually
  walk into unmapped memory. The `assert(id > 0 && id < js_class_count(rt))`
  guards in `js_class_atom`/`js_class_id` would normally catch a bad `id`
  afterwards, but this project builds with `-DNDEBUG` (CMakeLists.txt:873),
  so the assert compiles out and the out-of-bounds pointer arithmetic runs
  unchecked. This is the same class of fragility the project has been
  actively removing elsewhere (see 26d776f7, "remove QUICKJS_INTERNAL:
  ... struct-scraping header generation") - these four functions in
  src/utils.c look like leftover struct-scraping that wasn't migrated.
  tests/unittests/test-misc.js does not exercise classId/className/
  classAtom at all because of this.

    import * as misc from 'misc';
    misc.classId('Array'); misc.classId('Object'); misc.classId('X');
    // segfault, reliably within a handful of calls

- mmap-shared-flag-passed-as-arraybuffer-is-shared (quickjs-mmap.c:53):
  `mmap(addr, length, prot, MAP_SHARED, fd, offset)` segfaults 100%
  reproducibly (confirmed 3/3) as soon as the returned value is touched -
  `MAP_PRIVATE` mappings of the same file are fine. `js_mmap_map()` builds
  the result with
  `JS_NewArrayBuffer(ctx, ptr, length, &js_mmap_free_func, (void*)length,
  !!(flags & MAP_SHARED))` - that last argument is `JS_NewArrayBuffer`'s
  `is_shared` flag, which tells the engine this is backing storage for a
  cross-agent `SharedArrayBuffer` (with its own refcounting/atomics
  invariants), not "the OS-level mapping happens to be MAP_SHARED". Passing
  `flags & MAP_SHARED` straight through conflates the two, so any
  MAP_SHARED mmap silently gets engine-level SharedArrayBuffer semantics it
  was never set up for. Not fully root-caused past that (didn't trace where
  inside the engine it faults), but the flag is the obvious suspect since
  toggling only it (`MAP_PRIVATE` vs `MAP_SHARED`, same file/fd/prot)
  is what flips the crash on and off. Worked around in
  tests/unittests/test-mmap.js by using `MAP_PRIVATE` throughout, including
  for the `msync()`/`mprotect()` cases that would more naturally use
  `MAP_SHARED`.

    import * as os from 'os';
    import * as mmap from 'mmap';
    const fd = os.open('/etc/hostname', os.O_RDONLY);
    const map = mmap.mmap(null, 12, mmap.PROT_READ, mmap.MAP_SHARED, fd, 0);
    map.byteLength; // segfault

- pointer-compare-loses-direction (quickjs-pointer.c:587-602, pointer_compare
  in src/pointer.c): `Pointer.prototype.compare(other)` is documented as an
  "Ordering comparison" but its return value doesn't encode which side is
  greater - `['a'].compare(['b'])` and `['b'].compare(['a'])` both return
  `0`, so it can't be used to sort pointers by a differing first atom. What
  it does return looks like it's really `common()`'s internal encoding
  leaking out: equal pointers give a negative `-(commonLength)-1` (e.g.
  `['a'].compare(['a'])` -> -2, `['a','b'].compare(['a','b'])` -> -3, matching
  how METHOD_COMMON decodes `r < 0 ? -r - 1 : r`), and differing pointers
  give the non-negative common-prefix length instead of a signed ordering
  (`['a','b'].compare(['a','c'])` -> 1, not -1/0/1 in the position sense).
  100% reproducible. tests/unittests/test-pointer.js's compare() test only
  asserts the observed (not the documented) behavior because of this.

    import { Pointer } from 'pointer';
    new Pointer(['a']).compare(new Pointer(['b'])); // 0
    new Pointer(['b']).compare(new Pointer(['a'])); // 0 too - no direction

- predicate-bnot-sqrt-atan2-return-undefined (quickjs-predicate.c): calling
  `Predicate.bnot(x)`, `Predicate.sqrt(x)` or `Predicate.atan2(x, y)`
  returns `undefined` instead of a `Predicate`, even though all three are
  registered, exported, callable functions (`JS_CFUNC_MAGIC_DEF("bnot", ...
  PREDICATE_BNOT)` etc., quickjs-predicate.c:974/975/984) with working
  `toString`/inspect support for their PREDICATE_BNOT/SQRT/ATAN2 ids
  (quickjs-predicate.c:825-826,839). The actual builder switch inside
  `js_predicate_function()` (quickjs-predicate.c, the dispatcher every
  factory in doc/native/predicate.md goes through) simply has no
  `case PREDICATE_BNOT/SQRT/ATAN2:` arm - unlike every sibling arithmetic
  op (`add`/`sub`/`mul`/`div`/`mod`/`bor`/`band`/`pow`, all present), so the
  switch falls through doing nothing and the function returns its
  initial `ret = JS_UNDEFINED`. No exception is thrown, so this fails
  silently. Worked around in tests/unittests/test-predicate.js by not
  invoking `Predicate.bnot`/`sqrt`/`atan2`.

    import { Predicate } from 'predicate';
    Predicate.sqrt(9);           // undefined, expected a Predicate
    Predicate.sqrt(9)();         // TypeError: not a function

- predicate-xor-shares-args-cursor (src/predicate.c:196-208): `Predicate.xor
  (p1, p2, ...)` only evaluates its first sub-predicate against the actual
  call arguments; from the second sub-predicate onward it evaluates against
  stale/empty arguments, because the `PREDICATE_XOR` case in
  `predicate_eval()` passes the shared `args` (a stateful, shift-based
  `JSArguments` cursor) straight to `predicate_value()` on every loop
  iteration, whereas the otherwise-identical `PREDICATE_OR`/`PREDICATE_AND`
  cases just above it (src/predicate.c:172-194) each take a fresh
  `JSArguments args2 = *args` copy per iteration specifically to avoid this.
  Net effect: `Predicate.xor(Predicate.equal(1), Predicate.equal(1))(1)`
  (both operands should match -> logical/bitwise XOR of two trues -> falsy)
  instead evaluates as if only the first operand saw `1` and the second saw
  nothing, so it returns a truthy `1` in cases that should be falsy.
  Worked around in tests/unittests/test-predicate.js by only checking that
  `xor()` is callable and returns a number, not asserting specific
  true/false outcomes across multiple operands.

    import { Predicate } from 'predicate';
    Predicate.xor(Predicate.equal(1), Predicate.equal(1))(1);   // 1 (truthy), expected 0 (falsy)

- misc-dequote-does-not-remove-quotes (quickjs-misc.c:2627-2666):
  `misc.dequote(s)` is not the inverse of `misc.quote(s)`, despite
  doc/native/misc.md describing them as "Add / remove quoting". Both
  `quote` (magic 0) and `dequote` (magic 1) always wrap their output in
  the quote character (`dbuf_putc(&output, quote)` runs unconditionally
  before *and* after, for both magics) - `magic` only switches whether the
  interior bytes get escaped (`dbuf_put_escaped_table`) or unescaped
  (`dbuf_put_unescaped_table`). So `dequote('"a\\"b"')` unescapes the
  backslash but still adds a fresh pair of quote characters around the
  result, producing `"a"b""`-shaped output instead of stripping the
  surrounding quotes. tests/unittests/test-misc.js tests the real
  (always-wraps) behavior rather than the doc's "remove quoting"
  description.

    import * as misc from 'misc';
    misc.dequote(misc.quote('a"b'));   // '""a"b""', not 'a"b'

- misc-objectrefcount-struct-scraping (src/utils.c:1134-1162): same
  struct-scraping fragility as misc-classid-struct-scraping-segfault
  above, on a different pair of functions. `js_object_classid(v)` reads
  `((uint16_t*)p)[3]` and `js_object_refcount(v)` reads `((int*)p)[0]` off
  the object's raw `JSObject*` pointer, assuming a fixed private struct
  layout. On the currently vendored QuickJS these offsets are wrong:
  `misc.objectClassId({})` always returns 0 (a plain object's real class id
  is non-zero) instead of throwing or erroring, and `misc.objectRefCount({})`
  returns a different large, effectively-random int32 on every call -
  looks like it's reading a pointer field (varies with ASLR) rather than
  the refcount. Doesn't segfault (unlike the classId/className/classAtom
  case), just returns silently-wrong data, which is arguably worse since
  nothing signals the failure. tests/unittests/test-misc.js does not
  assert on the values of objectClassId()/objectRefCount(), only that
  they return a number.

    import * as misc from 'misc';
    misc.objectClassId({});   // 0, every time (should be a real class id)
    misc.objectRefCount({});  // a different huge number every run

- jsonserializer-read-throws-uninitialized-value (quickjs-json.c:1201-1214,
  1417-1420): `new JsonSerializer(value, indent).read(n)` throws a garbage,
  non-Error exception - `typeof e` is `"unknown"`, `e.constructor` and
  `e.message` are `undefined`, `e instanceof Error` is `false` - whenever
  `indent` is a nonzero number and `value` is a container (object or
  array); a primitive value, `indent` of `0`, or a string `indent` are all
  fine. Root cause: `sw_indent()` (quickjs-json.c:1201-1214, and the
  sibling `sw_string`/`sw_primitive`/etc. helpers at ~1176-1341) sets
  `js->error = TRUE` whenever `write_indent()`'s `skip_writer` probe
  returns negative, but never calls any `JS_Throw*` function on that path -
  `js_json_serializer_read()` (quickjs-json.c:1417-1420) then does
  `return JS_EXCEPTION;` with no exception ever having been set on `ctx`,
  so whatever was previously in `ctx->current_exception` (apparently an
  uninitialized/TDZ-sentinel JSValue on a fresh context) leaks out as the
  thrown value. Didn't root-cause why `write_indent`'s skip-writer probe
  returns negative in the first place for this input (that's the remaining
  open question - whether it's a legitimate OOM-style error being
  mishandled, or the probe itself is buggy for this indent+container
  combination).

  Found while investigating a user-reported
  "Possibly unhandled promise rejection: ReferenceError: arg is not
  initialized / at log (lib/console.js:100:52) / at run
  (lib/tinytest.js:62:22)" - that's this bug's corrupted exception value
  blowing up lib/console.js's `for(let arg of args)` loop (console.js:100)
  when tinytest.js:62 tries to log the "JsonSerializer: indent option
  matches write() options" test's failure in tests/unittests/test-json.js
  (a pre-existing test file, not one written by me) - the real bug is in
  quickjs-json.c, not in that test or in console.js/tinytest.js.

    import { JsonSerializer } from 'json';
    const s = new JsonSerializer({ a: 1, b: [2, 3] }, 2);
    s.read(1000);   // throws a non-Error; typeof result is "unknown"

- syscallerror-stack-always-null (quickjs-syscallerror.c:206, 289-292):
  `new SyscallError(syscall, errno).stack` is always `null`, 100%
  reproducible, despite doc/native/syscallerror.md documenting it as
  "Captured stack trace". The constructor unconditionally does
  `err->stack = js_error_stack(ctx);` (line 206) before returning, and
  `js_error_stack()` (src/utils.c:3341-3349, `JS_NewError` + read its
  `.stack` property) demonstrably produces a real, non-empty string when
  called the same way (`new Error('x').stack`) from plain top-level script
  in the same qjsm process - but whatever `js_syscallerror_constructor`
  ends up storing reads back as `null`/`undefined` through the `stack`
  getter (PROP_STACK case, quickjs-syscallerror.c:289-292, which converts
  a null-or-undefined `err->stack` to `JS_NULL`). Not root-caused further -
  didn't trace why `js_error_stack(ctx)` behaves differently when called
  from inside this native constructor vs. from script level (candidates:
  the engine's backtrace capture may depend on stack-frame state that
  differs for a `JS_CFUNC_constructor` call, or `.stack` may be a
  lazily-computed accessor whose capture point matters). Worked around in
  tests/unittests/test-syscallerror.js by asserting the actual (null)
  value instead of the documented (string) one.

    import { SyscallError } from 'syscallerror';
    const err = new SyscallError('open', SyscallError.ENOENT);
    err.stack;   // null, every run - should be a captured stack trace string

- lexer-unescape-doubles-backslash-instead-of-removing-it
  (quickjs-lexer.c:52-64): `Lexer.unescape()` is not the inverse of
  `Lexer.escape()` for metacharacters escape() itself produces (like
  `.`, `*`, `\`) - it doubles the backslash instead of consuming it.
  `escape_pred` (quickjs-lexer.c, just above) treats `. * + ? ( ) [ ] { }
  ^ $ | \ \r \n \t \v \f` as needing a leading backslash, but the inverse
  table, `unescape_pred` (quickjs-lexer.c:52-64), only maps the named
  control-character escapes (`\r \n \t \v \f \/`) back to their raw byte -
  it has no case for a backslash-escaped literal metacharacter, so
  `dbuf_put_unescaped_pred`'s fallback for an unrecognized escape emits
  both the backslash and the following character unchanged, instead of
  just the character. 100% reproducible, not flaky. Worked around in
  tests/unittests/test-lexer.js by only round-tripping escape()/unescape()
  through a string that contains none of `escape_pred`'s special
  characters.

    import { Lexer } from 'lexer';
    Lexer.escape('a.b*c');              // 'a\.b\*c' (correct)
    Lexer.unescape(Lexer.escape('a.b*c'));  // 'a\\.b\\*c', should be 'a.b*c'

- ecmascript-lexer-division-lexed-as-regexp (lib/lexer/ecmascript.js): no
  rule ever switches the lexer between INITIAL and NOREGEX, so a `/` after
  an identifier, number, `)` or `]` is first tried as a regexp literal (the
  regexpLiteral rule is listed before punctuator and the lexer is in FIRST
  mode). `a = b / c; // x` lexes `/ c; /` as one regexpLiteral, so the
  comment after it is swallowed and every later token is shifted. a
  consumer can work around it by setting `lexer.state = 'NOREGEX'` after a
  token that ends an expression (utilities/extract-comments.js does), but
  the lexer itself should do it, e.g. from rule callbacks.

    import ECMAScriptLexer from './lib/lexer/ecmascript.js';
    [...new ECMAScriptLexer('a = b / c; // x', 'f')].map(t => t.type);
    // [..., 'identifier', 'whitespace', 'regexpLiteral', 'punctuator', ...]
    // expected: identifier, punctuator '/', identifier, ..., comment

- ecmascript-lexer-division-in-template-substitution
  (lib/lexer/ecmascript.js): inside `${ ... }` the lexer is in the NESTED
  state, which allows a regexpLiteral and has no NOREGEX counterpart, so a
  division there cannot be told apart from a regexp even by a consumer that
  switches states (`lexer.state` is only safe in INITIAL/NOREGEX).

    const src = '`a${b / c}d${/r/.source}`';
    [...new ECMAScriptLexer(src, 'f')].map(t => t.lexeme);
    // the regexpLiteral lexeme is '/ c}d${/r', expected two separate tokens

- ecmascript-lexer-rejects-lone-dollar-in-template (lib/lexer/ecmascript.js,
  TemplateElement/TemplateStringCharacter): a `$` that is not followed by
  `{` is not accepted inside a template literal (the character class
  excludes `$`, and only `${` is handled), so the whole input fails with
  "No matching token". lib/css-selectors.js and lib/css3-selectors.js
  contain such a template (`^${part}$`), so tools that lex them (e.g.
  utilities/extract-comments.js) stop at that line.

    import ECMAScriptLexer from './lib/lexer/ecmascript.js';
    [...new ECMAScriptLexer('`a$b`', 'f')];
    // throws "No matching token (0: INITIAL)", expected one template token

- lexer-rule-expansion-never-freed (src/lexer.c: lexer_rule_compile ~146,
  lexer_rule_release_rt ~228): lexer_rule_compile() stores a js_strndup()ed
  copy of the expanded pattern in `rule->expansion`, but
  lexer_rule_release_rt() frees only name, expr and bytecode, so one copy
  of every compiled rule's pattern is kept until the runtime is freed.

- lexer-constructor-mode-argument-ignored (quickjs-lexer.c:694-697,
  src/lexer.c:247): lexer_new() starts `mode` at -1 (every bit set) and the
  constructor ORs the requested mode in (`lex->mode |= mode`), so
  `new Lexer(input, Lexer.FIRST)` and `new Lexer(input, Lexer.LONGEST)` both
  leave mode -1. Matching then behaves as longest-match, whatever was asked
  for. A later `lexer.mode = Lexer.FIRST` assignment (lib/lexer/bnf.js does
  it on a %directive) does switch to true first-match, so one lexer changes
  behaviour half way through its input. Not fixed because the ecmascript and
  bnf lexers declare FIRST and have only ever run as longest-match.

    import { Lexer } from 'lexer';
    new Lexer('x', Lexer.FIRST).mode;   // -1, expected 0

- lexer-skipbytes-overflows-charpos (quickjs-lexer.c:797-806): after
  consuming a token with `next()`/`nextToken()`, calling
  `lexer.skipBytes(n)` with a valid `n` (1 <= n <= the byte length of the
  token just consumed - the bound `skipBytes()` itself enforces, by
  comparing against `lex->byte_length`, i.e. the *current token's* length,
  not the remaining input) leaves `lexer.charPos` at 4294967296 (2^32) -
  an unsigned 32-bit wraparound, not a small, sensible cursor advance.
  100% reproducible (3/3 runs). Not root-caused past `js_lexer_method`'s
  `LEXER_SKIP_BYTES` case calling `lexer_skip_n(lex, n)`
  (quickjs-lexer.c:803) - didn't trace into `lexer_skip_n` itself (not
  found in a quick grep of src/lexer.c under that exact name). Separately,
  calling `skipBytes(n)` with `n` greater than the current token's byte
  length (including calling it right after construction, before any token
  has been scanned, when the "current token" length is 0) throws
  `InternalError: skipBytes(): count n > <byte_length>` - surprising given
  the doc table lists `skipBytes`/`skipChars` as taking 0 args, but not a
  bug once you know `n` is bounded by the current token, not the input.
  Worked around in tests/unittests/test-lexer.js by only calling
  skipBytes() with no argument (a harmless no-op) rather than exercising
  the overflow.

    import { Lexer } from 'lexer';
    const l = new Lexer('12 foo', Lexer.LONGEST, 'in.txt');
    l.addRule('number', /[0-9]+/);
    l.next();            // consumes "12", current token byte_length becomes 2
    l.skipBytes(1);      // valid (1 <= 2), but...
    l.charPos;            // 4294967296, not 3

- predicate-import-prints-typeerror-at-exit (quickjs-predicate.c): a script
  that only imports the predicate module prints "TypeError: not a function"
  to stderr after it finishes, with exit code 0. Seen on both the freshly
  built qjsm and the older installed one, so it predates recent changes.
  Not root-caused; likely module init or teardown. The location module does
  not do this.

    import { Predicate } from 'predicate';
    console.log(typeof Predicate);   // function, then: TypeError: not a function


- lexer-non-ascii-input-corrupts-tokens (src/lexer.c, quickjs-lexer.c): lexing
  source text that contains non-ascii (multi-byte utf-8) characters produces
  wrong tokens. the ecmascript lexer re-emits the tail of a block comment
  containing a non-ascii char (the comment lexeme comes out as "/* a - b */*/"),
  and the xml lexer returns a text token ending with the multi-byte chars
  followed by a bogus token whose lexeme is garbage ("�x") whose
  charLength is 0 and whose charOffset equals a byte count, so charOffset and
  byteOffset disagree about which unit they count. found while writing
  utilities/highlight.js, whose lossless round-trip check fails only on files
  with non-ascii text (examples/html2md.js, tests/test3.xml). ascii-only files
  round-trip exactly. not root-caused; lexer_set_location (src/lexer.c:480)
  assigns byte_offset from loc->char_offset, which may be related.

    import ECMAScriptLexer from './lib/lexer/ecmascript.js';
    [...new ECMAScriptLexer('/* a – b */\nlet x', 'f')].map(t => t.lexeme)[0];
    // "/* a – b */*/", expected "/* a – b */"

- pgsql-async-connect-live-server-hangs: an async pgsql connect (set
  pq.nonblocking = true, then pq.connect(...)) against a reachable server
  never settles cleanly. with deliberately wrong credentials against a local
  postgres on 127.0.0.1:5432 the promise resolves with undefined (not the
  connection, and not a rejection for the failed login), the then-callback
  fires repeatedly, and the process then hangs instead of exiting. an
  unreachable server now rejects correctly (js_pgconn_connect_cont handles
  PGRES_POLLING_FAILED), so the remaining defect is in the multi-step polling
  path of js_pgconn_connect_cont / js_pgconn_connect_start
  (quickjs-pgsql.c:1030-1125), which is not root-caused. found while checking
  that js_iohandler_fn works for pgsql. not tested with valid credentials.

    import { PGconn } from 'pgsql';
    const pq = new PGconn();
    pq.nonblocking = true;
    await pq.connect('127.0.0.1', 'nosuchuser', 'wrong', 'nosuchdb', 5432, 5);
    // expected: rejection with an authentication error
    // actual: resolves undefined; repeated callbacks; process hangs

- xmlparser-null-source-segfault (quickjs-xml.c): constructing an XMLParser
  with a null source and calling parse() crashes the process with a
